# NEVER REMOVED, in any mode, by anything in this directory.
#
# apply-policy resolves the removal transaction BEFORE executing it and aborts the build if the
# transaction would take any of these with it -- directly or as a dependency cascade. This is the
# trap D12 warns about from the other side: `dnf remove plasma-workspace` looks surgical right up
# until the closure decides systemd is no longer required by anything.
#
# A recipe that prunes its own update path produces a machine we can never patch again, which is
# precisely the abandoned laptop we sell against. Check S10 asserts the same set from outside; this
# file is what stops the image from being built in the first place.

# the update path -- lose this and the machine is orphaned, which is the thing we sell against
bootc
rpm-ostree
ostree
bootupd
greenboot
greenboot-default-health-checks
greenboot-grub2
grub2-common
grub2-efi-x64
shim-x64

# init, hardware, network
systemd
systemd-udev
systemd-resolved
kernel
kernel-core
kernel-modules
NetworkManager
dbus
dbus-broker
polkit
polkit-libs
sudo

# the security posture we ship
selinux-policy
selinux-policy-targeted
container-selinux

# what the kiosk session itself is made of
cage
weston
pipewire
wireplumber
xdg-desktop-portal
mesa-dri-drivers
mesa-vulkan-drivers

# the package manager itself -- removing it is the classic way a prune makes an image unbuildable
dnf
dnf5
rpm
